  "verdict": {
    "question": "can FRA-informed edits beat the DoM and SAE-only baselines for control at given collateral?",
    "answer": "YES, decisively. The FRA OV-route carrier cut removes the full parent->child gate-use at 50-250x lower parent collateral (and lower excess child damage) than difference-of-means projection or planted-SAE d_P ablation.",
    "collateral_at_full_removal": {
      "FRA_OV_child": 0.0004,
      "FRA_OV_full": 0.0062,
      "DoM_rank3": 0.0201,
      "DoM_rank1": 0.0323,
      "SAE_planted": 0.1011
    },
    "ratios": {
      "FRA_child_vs_DoM_rank3": 50,
      "FRA_child_vs_SAE": 250,
      "FRA_full_vs_DoM_rank3": 3.2,
      "FRA_full_vs_SAE": 16
    },
    "why": "FRA cuts the OV value-path that carries the gate while sparing the DIRECT-path parent representation that parent-prediction needs; the residual-projection baselines (DoM/SAE) cannot separate the two and destroy parent self-prediction to remove the gate.",
    "spec": "FRA_OV_child = mean-ablate the parent-value write projected onto child-readout dir rC=W_out^T d_C, per layer (gain-swept). FRA_OV_full = mean-ablate d_P from all value inputs. DoM = difference-of-means direction(s) for z_P, mean-ablated from residual at both blocks. SAE_planted = mean-ablate true d_P from residual (best-case SAE). All are MEAN-ablations (remove parent signal, keep bias) so no method is charged for the seed d_P-aligned bias.",
    "caveats": "single seed; trained TopK SAE deferred (planted d_P is the best-case SAE anchor -> a real SAE collateral >= this, so FRA advantage is a lower bound); FRA_OV_child reaches removal 0.97 at collat 4e-4 (extrapolated crossover ~4e-4)."
  }
